          "secNumber": "1.2",
          "heading": "Extended unit perspective",
          "content": "The first task in structural geology is to make geometrically explicit observations. A tilted bedding plane, a foliation, and an exposed fracture may all look like lines from a distance. Their geometries are planes in three-dimensional space, intersecting a weathered and eroded surface. In a field notebook, distinguish the orientation of an individual surface from the trace drawn on a horizontal map. A compass measurement is only one sample of that surface; natural surfaces are rough, anisotropic, and sometimes curved. Magnetic declination, dip quadrant, north reference and right-hand-rule conventions must accompany measurements if another person is expected to reproduce them. Combining data from separate structural domains into a single mean may conceal an important geological boundary. Most importantly, geometry is not process. A particular orientation pattern may be produced by folding, rotation about a fault, tilting during sedimentation, or several later deformation episodes. A useful structural map carries the observations separately from the reconstructed movement history. In this unit students should explicitly draw local x, y and z axes, show the horizontal projection of a line, and describe which rotations or projections convert observed directions into map symbols. A stereonet is an aid to comparing orientations, not a substitution for an actual stratigraphic section. Where an exposure is covered, mark missing evidence rather than drawing confident structure through it.\n\n### Field and scientific objectives\n\nDescribe relevant structural objects with location and orientation, translate them between map and section, construct a physically and chronologically consistent interpretation, and test at least one alternative. The aim is to communicate exactly which information is observed, which result follows by geometric calculation and which regional mechanism is inferred. Avoid interpreting one structural indicator as proof of a single tectonic event where earlier deformation, reactivation or anisotropic rock properties remain plausible.",
