          "heading": "Extended unit perspective",
          "content": "The distinction between motion and deformation prevents many structural interpretations from becoming circular. Two points can translate together by ten metres without any change in the distance between them, and therefore without strain. A material body can rotate without changing its shape, yet lineation orientations may change dramatically in the map reference frame. A strain ellipse captures final distortion but not the unique series of earlier increments that produced it. Stress is different again: it describes force per area acting on internal planes, and cannot be calculated from strain without assumptions about rheology and loading history. The same amount of shortening may form a narrow fault at one temperature and a distributed fold at another, because rock behaviour depends on pressure, fluid conditions, strain rate, lithology, and previous defects. Classroom Mohr circles simplify the stress tensor but remain useful for checking sign conventions and how normal and shear traction change as an imaginary plane rotates. The simple effective-stress relation reduces a normal stress by pore pressure; real pore systems, anisotropic permeability, rate dependence and thermal evolution complicate application. Lab reports must state whether an equation is kinematic, mechanical, empirical or merely a diagnostic ratio. Never claim a numerical stress inferred from fold wavelength alone without an independently justified constitutive model.\n\n### Field and scientific objectives\n\nDescribe relevant structural objects with location and orientation, translate them between map and section, construct a physically and chronologically consistent interpretation, and test at least one alternative. The aim is to communicate exactly which information is observed, which result follows by geometric calculation and which regional mechanism is inferred. Avoid interpreting one structural indicator as proof of a single tectonic event where earlier deformation, reactivation or anisotropic rock properties remain plausible.",
