          "content": "### Mechanism and observational scale\n\nA displacement vector records change from an initial to a final position in a defined reference frame; rigid translation produces displacement without strain. Draw a basic geometric sketch and define the coordinate system, sample orientation and reference state if one is needed. \n\n### Evidence and documentation\n\nMatched marker beds, veins, pebbles and survey points constrain relative movements over different time intervals. Document the exposure, instrument or measurement technique, scale and uncertainty before discussing tectonic implications. Maintain an observation/inference table with **reference state, principal axes and whether the formula is kinematic or mechanical** and illustrate **measured marker distortion, model stress and assumed material properties**. If your evidence is a two-dimensional trace of a three-dimensional surface, state exactly what its projection does and does not resolve. \n\n### Competing interpretation\n\nAn offset marker might result from differential erosion, stratigraphic lensing or bedding truncation rather than fault slip. Record expected results for each explanation before carrying out the test. A model requiring hidden geometry must mark that geometry as inferred.\n\n### Test and scientific protocol\n\nChoose two independent markers and check that their restored geometry yields consistent displacement vectors. State the predicted direction, location, quantity or material signature under both models. Use at least one measurement not previously employed to construct the model. For **displacement fields and kinematic reference frames**, define sample selection criteria, coordinate conventions and an acceptable range of observation error. \n\n### Worked numerical case 6: Marker displacement in two dimensions\n\n**Invented classroom inputs:** orthogonal marker components 12 and 5 m in fixed reference frame. Evaluate `math.hypot(12,5)` to obtain **13 m**. Write the governing equation separately, substitute the stated values, and check the dimensions and sign convention. For a trigonometric expression identify whether angles are entered in radians or degrees; for percentages identify the reference length or population. \n\nRepeat the calculation after varying the least certain input. Explain whether the change is important relative to plausible field error. If the formula neglects a deformation component, assumes a planar fault or uses a restricted stress model, identify that assumption rather than interpreting the number as exact. Distinguish geometric separation from true displacement, finite strain from its time rate and stress from material strength whenever those concepts arise in the calculation. \n\n### Limitation, cross-check and synthesis\n\nAbsolute reference positions are usually unknown for ancient structures and deformation may be nonuniform. Consider the alternative that changes the interpretation most while retaining the measured observations. Make a two-column field record. Put verifiable geometric and material facts in the left column and causal or historical interpretation in the right. For each interpreted event, cite the specific measured feature and its location. An interpretation without such a link is a hypothesis to test rather than a supported result. \n\nExplicitly distinguish a displacement vector from a strain measure. A defensible conclusion therefore has five components: observed feature, ideal relation used, leading interpretation, decisive contrary test and residual uncertainty. Indicate whether each is local to the measured exposure or can legitimately be extended across the mapped domain.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Sketch **displacement fields and kinematic reference frames** with orientation or movement arrows and a north or section reference.\n2. List three direct observations and identify a plausible rival mechanism.\n3. Recalculate case 6 for a revised parameter, retaining meaningful units.\n4. Design a two-site or two-orientation test that distinguishes the interpretations.\n5. Write a short final paragraph reporting what remains uncertain and which measurement would reduce the uncertainty.",
