          "heading": "Lesson 2.4 \u2014 Stress tensors and principal directions",
          "content": "### Mechanism and observational scale\n\nStress describes traction per unit area, and its tensor representation yields principal normal directions when shear components vanish in that rotated basis. Draw a basic geometric sketch and define the coordinate system, sample orientation and reference state if one is needed. \n\n### Evidence and documentation\n\nBreakout orientations, laboratory tests and some fault-slip inversion methods can constrain aspects of the stress field. Document the exposure, instrument or measurement technique, scale and uncertainty before discussing tectonic implications. Maintain an observation/inference table with **reference state, principal axes and whether the formula is kinematic or mechanical** and illustrate **measured marker distortion, model stress and assumed material properties**. If your evidence is a two-dimensional trace of a three-dimensional surface, state exactly what its projection does and does not resolve. \n\n### Competing interpretation\n\nA fold axis or fault strike is not automatically parallel to a principal stress direction in a heterogeneous crust. Record expected results for each explanation before carrying out the test. A model requiring hidden geometry must mark that geometry as inferred.\n\n### Test and scientific protocol\n\nDraw Mohr circles for an ideal two-dimensional state, rotate the plane and compare predicted normal and shear traction. State the predicted direction, location, quantity or material signature under both models. Use at least one measurement not previously employed to construct the model. For **stress tensors and principal directions**, define sample selection criteria, coordinate conventions and an acceptable range of observation error. \n\n### Worked numerical case 9: Differential principal stress\n\n**Invented classroom inputs:** illustrative principal stresses of 115 and 54 MPa. Evaluate `115-54` to obtain **61 MPa**. Write the governing equation separately, substitute the stated values, and check the dimensions and sign convention. For a trigonometric expression identify whether angles are entered in radians or degrees; for percentages identify the reference length or population. \n\nRepeat the calculation after varying the least certain input. Explain whether the change is important relative to plausible field error. If the formula neglects a deformation component, assumes a planar fault or uses a restricted stress model, identify that assumption rather than interpreting the number as exact. Distinguish geometric separation from true displacement, finite strain from its time rate and stress from material strength whenever those concepts arise in the calculation. \n\n### Limitation, cross-check and synthesis\n\nAncient stress inference is nonunique and stress directions can evolve during deformation. Consider the alternative that changes the interpretation most while retaining the measured observations. Translate the interpretation between a map, a cross-section and a three-dimensional conceptual diagram. Check that all three representations show compatible orientations and movement directions. A model that works in one section but fails in map view needs revision. \n\nSeparate model-imposed principal stresses from independently observed fault or fabric geometry. A defensible conclusion therefore has five components: observed feature, ideal relation used, leading interpretation, decisive contrary test and residual uncertainty. Indicate whether each is local to the measured exposure or can legitimately be extended across the mapped domain.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Sketch **stress tensors and principal directions** with orientation or movement arrows and a north or section reference.\n2. List three direct observations and identify a plausible rival mechanism.\n3. Recalculate case 9 for a revised parameter, retaining meaningful units.\n4. Design a two-site or two-orientation test that distinguishes the interpretations.\n5. Write a short final paragraph reporting what remains uncertain and which measurement would reduce the uncertainty.",
