          "heading": "Lesson 2.5 \u2014 Failure, friction and pore-pressure effects",
          "content": "### Mechanism and observational scale\n\nOn a preexisting cohesion-bearing surface, a simple Coulomb criterion compares shear traction with cohesion plus friction times effective normal stress. Draw a basic geometric sketch and define the coordinate system, sample orientation and reference state if one is needed. \n\n### Evidence and documentation\n\nFault-gouge mineralogy, vein fillings and slip surfaces together constrain material and fluid influence on deformation. Document the exposure, instrument or measurement technique, scale and uncertainty before discussing tectonic implications. Maintain an observation/inference table with **reference state, principal axes and whether the formula is kinematic or mechanical** and illustrate **measured marker distortion, model stress and assumed material properties**. If your evidence is a two-dimensional trace of a three-dimensional surface, state exactly what its projection does and does not resolve. \n\n### Competing interpretation\n\nHigh pore pressure can reduce effective normal stress, but the same fracture orientation may arise under a different total stress state. Record expected results for each explanation before carrying out the test. A model requiring hidden geometry must mark that geometry as inferred.\n\n### Test and scientific protocol\n\nVary pore pressure and coefficient of friction in a simplified calculation; compare the resulting threshold with observed failure only within the assumptions. State the predicted direction, location, quantity or material signature under both models. Use at least one measurement not previously employed to construct the model. For **failure, friction and pore-pressure effects**, define sample selection criteria, coordinate conventions and an acceptable range of observation error. \n\n### Worked numerical case 10: Idealized Coulomb shear threshold\n\n**Invented classroom inputs:** cohesion 12 MPa, friction 0.58, normal stress 87 MPa, pore pressure 32 MPa. Evaluate `12+0.58*(87-32)` to obtain **43.9 MPa**. Write the governing equation separately, substitute the stated values, and check the dimensions and sign convention. For a trigonometric expression identify whether angles are entered in radians or degrees; for percentages identify the reference length or population. \n\nRepeat the calculation after varying the least certain input. Explain whether the change is important relative to plausible field error. If the formula neglects a deformation component, assumes a planar fault or uses a restricted stress model, identify that assumption rather than interpreting the number as exact. Distinguish geometric separation from true displacement, finite strain from its time rate and stress from material strength whenever those concepts arise in the calculation. \n\n### Limitation, cross-check and synthesis\n\nReal faults have rate, temperature, mineralogy, roughness and fluid feedbacks absent from a linear criterion. Consider the alternative that changes the interpretation most while retaining the measured observations. List which surfaces or fabrics predate which others using direct cut, fold, overprint or growth relations. Leave pairs unordered where no observation establishes priority. Reactivation means a single surface may occur more than once in the event graph. \n\nTreat Coulomb calculations as conceptual constraints, never site-specific failure predictions. A defensible conclusion therefore has five components: observed feature, ideal relation used, leading interpretation, decisive contrary test and residual uncertainty. Indicate whether each is local to the measured exposure or can legitimately be extended across the mapped domain.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Sketch **failure, friction and pore-pressure effects** with orientation or movement arrows and a north or section reference.\n2. List three direct observations and identify a plausible rival mechanism.\n3. Recalculate case 10 for a revised parameter, retaining meaningful units.\n4. Design a two-site or two-orientation test that distinguishes the interpretations.\n5. Write a short final paragraph reporting what remains uncertain and which measurement would reduce the uncertainty.",
