          "secNumber": "3.2",
          "heading": "Extended unit perspective",
          "content": "Folds are not merely a series of anticline and syncline symbols on a map. A fold has a geometry in three dimensions and a history that may vary along its hinge. It can be upright or overturned, gently or tightly closed, cylindrical or changing plunge. Recognition of bed younging is essential when deciding whether the older rocks occur in the centre of a structure. In a complete structural report, record the orientation of bedding, facing, cleavage and lineation separately, then sketch how each would be observed on the two limbs. Remember that a map trace can look like a closure simply because the contact intersects topography. Likewise, an asymmetric fold may indicate vergence but not by itself give an unambiguous stress axis. Mechanics enter through competence contrasts and deformation partitioning: buckle folds, flexural-slip folds and passive folds do not preserve bed thickness and internal markers in the same way. Apparent bed-thickness changes should be checked using measurements perpendicular to layering rather than along a conveniently exposed surface. Where a second cleavage cuts an earlier fold, relative timing matters more than a textbook classification of interference shape. The unit's mathematical problems are small pieces of an overall testable cross-section; their purpose is to flag inconsistent geometry rather than reconstruct a unique regional history.\n\n### Field and scientific objectives\n\nDescribe relevant structural objects with location and orientation, translate them between map and section, construct a physically and chronologically consistent interpretation, and test at least one alternative. The aim is to communicate exactly which information is observed, which result follows by geometric calculation and which regional mechanism is inferred. Avoid interpreting one structural indicator as proof of a single tectonic event where earlier deformation, reactivation or anisotropic rock properties remain plausible.",
