          "content": "### Mechanism and observational scale\n\nUpright, inclined, recumbent, plunging and noncylindrical folds are geometric descriptions sensitive to the chosen reference plane and line. Draw a basic geometric sketch and define the coordinate system, sample orientation and reference state if one is needed. \n\n### Evidence and documentation\n\nMap closures, hinge line trajectories and interlimb angles distinguish simple cylindrical cartoons from variable real folds. Document the exposure, instrument or measurement technique, scale and uncertainty before discussing tectonic implications. Maintain an observation/inference table with **stratigraphic younging, projected section azimuth and hinge-axis continuity** and illustrate **mapped limbs, possible interference and inferred hinge geometry**. If your evidence is a two-dimensional trace of a three-dimensional surface, state exactly what its projection does and does not resolve. \n\n### Competing interpretation\n\nA map closure can also be produced by a dome, basin, topographic relief or fault-bounded repetition. Record expected results for each explanation before carrying out the test. A model requiring hidden geometry must mark that geometry as inferred.\n\n### Test and scientific protocol\n\nCompare a fold-axis prediction from poles to bedding with independently measured hinge orientations along strike. State the predicted direction, location, quantity or material signature under both models. Use at least one measurement not previously employed to construct the model. For **fold classification and three-dimensional variation**, define sample selection criteria, coordinate conventions and an acceptable range of observation error. \n\n### Worked numerical case 12: Interlimb angular complement\n\n**Invented classroom inputs:** 116\u00b0 angle between directed limb tangents in a constructed section. Evaluate `180-116` to obtain **64 degrees**. Write the governing equation separately, substitute the stated values, and check the dimensions and sign convention. For a trigonometric expression identify whether angles are entered in radians or degrees; for percentages identify the reference length or population. \n\nRepeat the calculation after varying the least certain input. Explain whether the change is important relative to plausible field error. If the formula neglects a deformation component, assumes a planar fault or uses a restricted stress model, identify that assumption rather than interpreting the number as exact. Distinguish geometric separation from true displacement, finite strain from its time rate and stress from material strength whenever those concepts arise in the calculation. \n\n### Limitation, cross-check and synthesis\n\nA single stereonet of mixed domains can mask progressive changes in axis plunge. Consider the alternative that changes the interpretation most while retaining the measured observations. Write two incompatible predictions *before* inspecting another observation. Make one prediction spatial, such as a contact location or expected change in orientation, and the other material, such as fault rock or crosscutting microstructure. Explain which result could falsify the leading model. \n\nUse classification as a compact description, not an explanation of force history. A defensible conclusion therefore has five components: observed feature, ideal relation used, leading interpretation, decisive contrary test and residual uncertainty. Indicate whether each is local to the measured exposure or can legitimately be extended across the mapped domain.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Sketch **fold classification and three-dimensional variation** with orientation or movement arrows and a north or section reference.\n2. List three direct observations and identify a plausible rival mechanism.\n3. Recalculate case 12 for a revised parameter, retaining meaningful units.\n4. Design a two-site or two-orientation test that distinguishes the interpretations.\n5. Write a short final paragraph reporting what remains uncertain and which measurement would reduce the uncertainty.",
