          "secNumber": "4.3",
          "heading": "Lesson 4.1 \u2014 Normal, reverse and thrust faults",
          "content": "### Mechanism and observational scale\n\nThe relative displacement of hanging wall and footwall along a dipping fault plane distinguishes normal from reverse separation when true slip is constrained. Draw a basic geometric sketch and define the coordinate system, sample orientation and reference state if one is needed. \n\n### Evidence and documentation\n\nCutoff lines, drag folds, fault-bend geometry and matched marker units reveal displacement across a fault. Document the exposure, instrument or measurement technique, scale and uncertainty before discussing tectonic implications. Maintain an observation/inference table with **rake convention, fault plane orientation and reliability of each piercing marker** and illustrate **measured separation, decomposed slip and restoration choice**. If your evidence is a two-dimensional trace of a three-dimensional surface, state exactly what its projection does and does not resolve. \n\n### Competing interpretation\n\nApparent stratigraphic omission can also result from an unconformity, and repetition can reflect folding rather than thrusting. Record expected results for each explanation before carrying out the test. A model requiring hidden geometry must mark that geometry as inferred.\n\n### Test and scientific protocol\n\nMap both sides, estimate three-dimensional slip direction, and reconstruct marker continuity under explicit hanging-wall geometry. State the predicted direction, location, quantity or material signature under both models. Use at least one measurement not previously employed to construct the model. For **normal, reverse and thrust faults**, define sample selection criteria, coordinate conventions and an acceptable range of observation error. \n\n### Worked numerical case 16: Fault-plane throw from heave\n\n**Invented classroom inputs:** ideal fault at 40\u00b0 dip with horizontal separation 38 m; simple sectional geometry. Evaluate `38*math.tan(math.radians(40))` to obtain **31.8858 m**. Write the governing equation separately, substitute the stated values, and check the dimensions and sign convention. For a trigonometric expression identify whether angles are entered in radians or degrees; for percentages identify the reference length or population. \n\nRepeat the calculation after varying the least certain input. Explain whether the change is important relative to plausible field error. If the formula neglects a deformation component, assumes a planar fault or uses a restricted stress model, identify that assumption rather than interpreting the number as exact. Distinguish geometric separation from true displacement, finite strain from its time rate and stress from material strength whenever those concepts arise in the calculation. \n\n### Limitation, cross-check and synthesis\n\nFault dip may vary at depth and growth strata may record several phases of motion. Consider the alternative that changes the interpretation most while retaining the measured observations. Make a two-column field record. Put verifiable geometric and material facts in the left column and causal or historical interpretation in the right. For each interpreted event, cite the specific measured feature and its location. An interpretation without such a link is a hypothesis to test rather than a supported result. \n\nUse multiple offset markers to determine actual movement instead of naming a fault from one section. A defensible conclusion therefore has five components: observed feature, ideal relation used, leading interpretation, decisive contrary test and residual uncertainty. Indicate whether each is local to the measured exposure or can legitimately be extended across the mapped domain.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Sketch **normal, reverse and thrust faults** with orientation or movement arrows and a north or section reference.\n2. List three direct observations and identify a plausible rival mechanism.\n3. Recalculate case 16 for a revised parameter, retaining meaningful units.\n4. Design a two-site or two-orientation test that distinguishes the interpretations.\n5. Write a short final paragraph reporting what remains uncertain and which measurement would reduce the uncertainty.",
