          "secNumber": "4.4",
          "heading": "Lesson 4.2 \u2014 Strike-slip fault systems and stepovers",
          "content": "### Mechanism and observational scale\n\nLateral slip occurs mainly along the strike of a near-vertical plane, with releasing or restraining bends arising from the combination of sense and bend geometry. Draw a basic geometric sketch and define the coordinate system, sample orientation and reference state if one is needed. \n\n### Evidence and documentation\n\nOffset streams, piercing points, en echelon folds and pull-apart basins constrain kinematics in well-exposed systems. Document the exposure, instrument or measurement technique, scale and uncertainty before discussing tectonic implications. Maintain an observation/inference table with **rake convention, fault plane orientation and reliability of each piercing marker** and illustrate **measured separation, decomposed slip and restoration choice**. If your evidence is a two-dimensional trace of a three-dimensional surface, state exactly what its projection does and does not resolve. \n\n### Competing interpretation\n\nA river bend may predate a fault, and a single offset channel can be abandoned or modified by erosion. Record expected results for each explanation before carrying out the test. A model requiring hidden geometry must mark that geometry as inferred.\n\n### Test and scientific protocol\n\nCompare the lateral shift of two independent pre-slip piercing features and evaluate whether inferred slip agrees in magnitude and sign. State the predicted direction, location, quantity or material signature under both models. Use at least one measurement not previously employed to construct the model. For **strike-slip fault systems and stepovers**, define sample selection criteria, coordinate conventions and an acceptable range of observation error. \n\n### Worked numerical case 17: Net horizontal strike-slip offset\n\n**Invented classroom inputs:** orthogonal reconstructed offsets 24 and 7 m. Evaluate `math.hypot(24,7)` to obtain **25 m**. Write the governing equation separately, substitute the stated values, and check the dimensions and sign convention. For a trigonometric expression identify whether angles are entered in radians or degrees; for percentages identify the reference length or population. \n\nRepeat the calculation after varying the least certain input. Explain whether the change is important relative to plausible field error. If the formula neglects a deformation component, assumes a planar fault or uses a restricted stress model, identify that assumption rather than interpreting the number as exact. Distinguish geometric separation from true displacement, finite strain from its time rate and stress from material strength whenever those concepts arise in the calculation. \n\n### Limitation, cross-check and synthesis\n\nVertical components and distributed deformation can complicate a simple horizontal-offset estimate. Consider the alternative that changes the interpretation most while retaining the measured observations. Write two incompatible predictions *before* inspecting another observation. Make one prediction spatial, such as a contact location or expected change in orientation, and the other material, such as fault rock or crosscutting microstructure. Explain which result could falsify the leading model. \n\nState whether a measurement is apparent separation, net slip or a geomorphic offset. A defensible conclusion therefore has five components: observed feature, ideal relation used, leading interpretation, decisive contrary test and residual uncertainty. Indicate whether each is local to the measured exposure or can legitimately be extended across the mapped domain.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Sketch **strike-slip fault systems and stepovers** with orientation or movement arrows and a north or section reference.\n2. List three direct observations and identify a plausible rival mechanism.\n3. Recalculate case 17 for a revised parameter, retaining meaningful units.\n4. Design a two-site or two-orientation test that distinguishes the interpretations.\n5. Write a short final paragraph reporting what remains uncertain and which measurement would reduce the uncertainty.",
