          "heading": "Lesson 4.3 \u2014 Rake, slickensides and oblique slip",
          "content": "### Mechanism and observational scale\n\nRake is an angle measured within a plane relative to a reference direction, allowing a measured net slip vector to be decomposed into strike and dip components. Draw a basic geometric sketch and define the coordinate system, sample orientation and reference state if one is needed. \n\n### Evidence and documentation\n\nPolished steps, mineral fibres and offset lineations can combine to constrain slip direction and, sometimes, sense. Document the exposure, instrument or measurement technique, scale and uncertainty before discussing tectonic implications. Maintain an observation/inference table with **rake convention, fault plane orientation and reliability of each piercing marker** and illustrate **measured separation, decomposed slip and restoration choice**. If your evidence is a two-dimensional trace of a three-dimensional surface, state exactly what its projection does and does not resolve. \n\n### Competing interpretation\n\nSlickenside grooves can record the most recent slip rather than the total displacement of an older fault. Record expected results for each explanation before carrying out the test. A model requiring hidden geometry must mark that geometry as inferred.\n\n### Test and scientific protocol\n\nCompute strike-parallel and dip-parallel components from a known slip and rake, then compare with independently restored marker offsets. State the predicted direction, location, quantity or material signature under both models. Use at least one measurement not previously employed to construct the model. For **rake, slickensides and oblique slip**, define sample selection criteria, coordinate conventions and an acceptable range of observation error. \n\n### Worked numerical case 18: Strike component of net slip\n\n**Invented classroom inputs:** net slip 60 m, rake 28\u00b0 measured from strike within fault plane. Evaluate `60*math.cos(math.radians(28))` to obtain **52.9769 m**. Write the governing equation separately, substitute the stated values, and check the dimensions and sign convention. For a trigonometric expression identify whether angles are entered in radians or degrees; for percentages identify the reference length or population. \n\nRepeat the calculation after varying the least certain input. Explain whether the change is important relative to plausible field error. If the formula neglects a deformation component, assumes a planar fault or uses a restricted stress model, identify that assumption rather than interpreting the number as exact. Distinguish geometric separation from true displacement, finite strain from its time rate and stress from material strength whenever those concepts arise in the calculation. \n\n### Limitation, cross-check and synthesis\n\nRake conventions and lineation polarity must be specified; unknown polarity prevents a unique sense determination. Consider the alternative that changes the interpretation most while retaining the measured observations. Identify the least certain input, vary it by a physically plausible amount, and show its effect on the computed result. Variation in a trigonometric angle may produce a nonlinear uncertainty even where angle readings appear precise. Report significant figures in proportion to the instrument and assumptions. \n\nReconstruct oblique slip with component vectors and uncertainty rather than a single apparent offset. A defensible conclusion therefore has five components: observed feature, ideal relation used, leading interpretation, decisive contrary test and residual uncertainty. Indicate whether each is local to the measured exposure or can legitimately be extended across the mapped domain.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Sketch **rake, slickensides and oblique slip** with orientation or movement arrows and a north or section reference.\n2. List three direct observations and identify a plausible rival mechanism.\n3. Recalculate case 18 for a revised parameter, retaining meaningful units.\n4. Design a two-site or two-orientation test that distinguishes the interpretations.\n5. Write a short final paragraph reporting what remains uncertain and which measurement would reduce the uncertainty.",
