          "heading": "Lesson 4.4 \u2014 Fault rocks, gouge and damage zones",
          "content": "### Mechanism and observational scale\n\nSlip may localize in cataclasite, gouge or foliated fault rock while fracture density and permeability vary through a wider damage zone. Draw a basic geometric sketch and define the coordinate system, sample orientation and reference state if one is needed. \n\n### Evidence and documentation\n\nClast angularity, grain size, vein cutting relations and localized shear fabrics record fault-zone processes. Document the exposure, instrument or measurement technique, scale and uncertainty before discussing tectonic implications. Maintain an observation/inference table with **rake convention, fault plane orientation and reliability of each piercing marker** and illustrate **measured separation, decomposed slip and restoration choice**. If your evidence is a two-dimensional trace of a three-dimensional surface, state exactly what its projection does and does not resolve. \n\n### Competing interpretation\n\nA broad fractured zone may be a joint corridor or lithologic contrast, not necessarily an active fault core. Record expected results for each explanation before carrying out the test. A model requiring hidden geometry must mark that geometry as inferred.\n\n### Test and scientific protocol\n\nMeasure fracture intensity transects across an interpreted core and document lithology and weathering controls. State the predicted direction, location, quantity or material signature under both models. Use at least one measurement not previously employed to construct the model. For **fault rocks, gouge and damage zones**, define sample selection criteria, coordinate conventions and an acceptable range of observation error. \n\n### Worked numerical case 19: Damage-zone fracture count density\n\n**Invented classroom inputs:** 74 fractures along 18.5 m scanline perpendicular to set. Evaluate `74/18.5` to obtain **4 count/m**. Write the governing equation separately, substitute the stated values, and check the dimensions and sign convention. For a trigonometric expression identify whether angles are entered in radians or degrees; for percentages identify the reference length or population. \n\nRepeat the calculation after varying the least certain input. Explain whether the change is important relative to plausible field error. If the formula neglects a deformation component, assumes a planar fault or uses a restricted stress model, identify that assumption rather than interpreting the number as exact. Distinguish geometric separation from true displacement, finite strain from its time rate and stress from material strength whenever those concepts arise in the calculation. \n\n### Limitation, cross-check and synthesis\n\nWeathering and exposure bias can overstate damage width or obscure distributed ductile shear. Consider the alternative that changes the interpretation most while retaining the measured observations. Translate the interpretation between a map, a cross-section and a three-dimensional conceptual diagram. Check that all three representations show compatible orientations and movement directions. A model that works in one section but fails in map view needs revision. \n\nFault-zone classification must integrate microstructures, spatial patterns and mapped displacement. A defensible conclusion therefore has five components: observed feature, ideal relation used, leading interpretation, decisive contrary test and residual uncertainty. Indicate whether each is local to the measured exposure or can legitimately be extended across the mapped domain.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Sketch **fault rocks, gouge and damage zones** with orientation or movement arrows and a north or section reference.\n2. List three direct observations and identify a plausible rival mechanism.\n3. Recalculate case 19 for a revised parameter, retaining meaningful units.\n4. Design a two-site or two-orientation test that distinguishes the interpretations.\n5. Write a short final paragraph reporting what remains uncertain and which measurement would reduce the uncertainty.",
