          "heading": "Lesson 4.5 \u2014 Fault growth, linkage and restoration",
          "content": "### Mechanism and observational scale\n\nFault segments grow, interact and can link, changing displacement distributions through time; balanced restoration enforces geometry and material conservation assumptions. Draw a basic geometric sketch and define the coordinate system, sample orientation and reference state if one is needed. \n\n### Evidence and documentation\n\nDisplacement\u2013distance profiles, relay ramps, growth strata and fault tip geometries test histories of segment propagation. Document the exposure, instrument or measurement technique, scale and uncertainty before discussing tectonic implications. Maintain an observation/inference table with **rake convention, fault plane orientation and reliability of each piercing marker** and illustrate **measured separation, decomposed slip and restoration choice**. If your evidence is a two-dimensional trace of a three-dimensional surface, state exactly what its projection does and does not resolve. \n\n### Competing interpretation\n\nA displacement low may reflect missing data or an unrecognized cross fault rather than a genuine relay segment. Record expected results for each explanation before carrying out the test. A model requiring hidden geometry must mark that geometry as inferred.\n\n### Test and scientific protocol\n\nTrace slip markers at several elevations, restore sequential cross-sections and note where bed-length balance fails. State the predicted direction, location, quantity or material signature under both models. Use at least one measurement not previously employed to construct the model. For **fault growth, linkage and restoration**, define sample selection criteria, coordinate conventions and an acceptable range of observation error. \n\n### Worked numerical case 20: Maximum measured displacement change\n\n**Invented classroom inputs:** mapped marker separations along the same fault change from 11 to 46 m. Evaluate `46-11` to obtain **35 m**. Write the governing equation separately, substitute the stated values, and check the dimensions and sign convention. For a trigonometric expression identify whether angles are entered in radians or degrees; for percentages identify the reference length or population. \n\nRepeat the calculation after varying the least certain input. Explain whether the change is important relative to plausible field error. If the formula neglects a deformation component, assumes a planar fault or uses a restricted stress model, identify that assumption rather than interpreting the number as exact. Distinguish geometric separation from true displacement, finite strain from its time rate and stress from material strength whenever those concepts arise in the calculation. \n\n### Limitation, cross-check and synthesis\n\nRestoration assumes specific deformation mechanisms and may be nonunique even when perfectly balanced. Consider the alternative that changes the interpretation most while retaining the measured observations. List which surfaces or fabrics predate which others using direct cut, fold, overprint or growth relations. Leave pairs unordered where no observation establishes priority. Reactivation means a single surface may occur more than once in the event graph. \n\nTreat balancing as a necessary geometric check under assumptions, not proof of one tectonic history. A defensible conclusion therefore has five components: observed feature, ideal relation used, leading interpretation, decisive contrary test and residual uncertainty. Indicate whether each is local to the measured exposure or can legitimately be extended across the mapped domain.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Sketch **fault growth, linkage and restoration** with orientation or movement arrows and a north or section reference.\n2. List three direct observations and identify a plausible rival mechanism.\n3. Recalculate case 20 for a revised parameter, retaining meaningful units.\n4. Design a two-site or two-orientation test that distinguishes the interpretations.\n5. Write a short final paragraph reporting what remains uncertain and which measurement would reduce the uncertainty.",
