          "secNumber": "5.5",
          "heading": "Lesson 5.3 \u2014 Vein arrays and crack-seal chronology",
          "content": "### Mechanism and observational scale\n\nMineral precipitation can fill fractures during successive episodes of opening, fluid flow and sealing, generating layered or fibrous veins. Draw a basic geometric sketch and define the coordinate system, sample orientation and reference state if one is needed. \n\n### Evidence and documentation\n\nCross-cutting veins, inclusion trails and growth zoning provide relative timing even without numerical ages. Document the exposure, instrument or measurement technique, scale and uncertainty before discussing tectonic implications. Maintain an observation/inference table with **scanline orientation, surface exposure window and relative age of mineral fill** and illustrate **joint set frequency, intersection topology and hydraulic uncertainty**. If your evidence is a two-dimensional trace of a three-dimensional surface, state exactly what its projection does and does not resolve. \n\n### Competing interpretation\n\nA broad vein may form by one opening pulse instead of many, and intersecting veins may reactivate. Record expected results for each explanation before carrying out the test. A model requiring hidden geometry must mark that geometry as inferred.\n\n### Test and scientific protocol\n\nBuild a paragenetic order from inclusion bands and multiple intersections and test whether it is internally transitive. State the predicted direction, location, quantity or material signature under both models. Use at least one measurement not previously employed to construct the model. For **vein arrays and crack-seal chronology**, define sample selection criteria, coordinate conventions and an acceptable range of observation error. \n\n### Worked numerical case 23: Vein opening ratio to host thickness\n\n**Invented classroom inputs:** 4.2 mm vein aperture relative to a 250 mm host segment. Evaluate `100*4.2/250` to obtain **1.68 %**. Write the governing equation separately, substitute the stated values, and check the dimensions and sign convention. For a trigonometric expression identify whether angles are entered in radians or degrees; for percentages identify the reference length or population. \n\nRepeat the calculation after varying the least certain input. Explain whether the change is important relative to plausible field error. If the formula neglects a deformation component, assumes a planar fault or uses a restricted stress model, identify that assumption rather than interpreting the number as exact. Distinguish geometric separation from true displacement, finite strain from its time rate and stress from material strength whenever those concepts arise in the calculation. \n\n### Limitation, cross-check and synthesis\n\nDifferent minerals may nucleate at different times and temperature regimes; age of host rock is not age of vein. Consider the alternative that changes the interpretation most while retaining the measured observations. Identify the least certain input, vary it by a physically plausible amount, and show its effect on the computed result. Variation in a trigonometric angle may produce a nonlinear uncertainty even where angle readings appear precise. Report significant figures in proportion to the instrument and assumptions. \n\nRecord a defensible sequence of events, not an unwarranted single event date. A defensible conclusion therefore has five components: observed feature, ideal relation used, leading interpretation, decisive contrary test and residual uncertainty. Indicate whether each is local to the measured exposure or can legitimately be extended across the mapped domain.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Sketch **vein arrays and crack-seal chronology** with orientation or movement arrows and a north or section reference.\n2. List three direct observations and identify a plausible rival mechanism.\n3. Recalculate case 23 for a revised parameter, retaining meaningful units.\n4. Design a two-site or two-orientation test that distinguishes the interpretations.\n5. Write a short final paragraph reporting what remains uncertain and which measurement would reduce the uncertainty.",
