          "secNumber": "5.6",
          "heading": "Lesson 5.4 \u2014 Fracture connectivity and permeability",
          "content": "### Mechanism and observational scale\n\nA fracture network transmits fluid according to aperture, connectivity, stress state and sealing; fracture count alone does not specify permeability. Draw a basic geometric sketch and define the coordinate system, sample orientation and reference state if one is needed. \n\n### Evidence and documentation\n\nIntersections, terminations, mineral fill and directional hydraulic responses help identify connected pathways. Document the exposure, instrument or measurement technique, scale and uncertainty before discussing tectonic implications. Maintain an observation/inference table with **scanline orientation, surface exposure window and relative age of mineral fill** and illustrate **joint set frequency, intersection topology and hydraulic uncertainty**. If your evidence is a two-dimensional trace of a three-dimensional surface, state exactly what its projection does and does not resolve. \n\n### Competing interpretation\n\nTwo rocks with identical fracture density can have radically different transport if one is sealed or poorly connected. Record expected results for each explanation before carrying out the test. A model requiring hidden geometry must mark that geometry as inferred.\n\n### Test and scientific protocol\n\nBuild a graph of fracture intersections and test sensitivity when short discontinuous branches are omitted. State the predicted direction, location, quantity or material signature under both models. Use at least one measurement not previously employed to construct the model. For **fracture connectivity and permeability**, define sample selection criteria, coordinate conventions and an acceptable range of observation error. \n\n### Worked numerical case 24: Connected-node percentage\n\n**Invented classroom inputs:** 34 mapped multi-arm intersections and 19 dead ends in schematic network. Evaluate `100*34/(34+19)` to obtain **64.1509 %**. Write the governing equation separately, substitute the stated values, and check the dimensions and sign convention. For a trigonometric expression identify whether angles are entered in radians or degrees; for percentages identify the reference length or population. \n\nRepeat the calculation after varying the least certain input. Explain whether the change is important relative to plausible field error. If the formula neglects a deformation component, assumes a planar fault or uses a restricted stress model, identify that assumption rather than interpreting the number as exact. Distinguish geometric separation from true displacement, finite strain from its time rate and stress from material strength whenever those concepts arise in the calculation. \n\n### Limitation, cross-check and synthesis\n\nA two-dimensional pavement map may misrepresent the three-dimensional percolating network. Consider the alternative that changes the interpretation most while retaining the measured observations. Translate the interpretation between a map, a cross-section and a three-dimensional conceptual diagram. Check that all three representations show compatible orientations and movement directions. A model that works in one section but fails in map view needs revision. \n\nTreat network geometry and hydraulic transmissivity as distinct measurements. A defensible conclusion therefore has five components: observed feature, ideal relation used, leading interpretation, decisive contrary test and residual uncertainty. Indicate whether each is local to the measured exposure or can legitimately be extended across the mapped domain.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Sketch **fracture connectivity and permeability** with orientation or movement arrows and a north or section reference.\n2. List three direct observations and identify a plausible rival mechanism.\n3. Recalculate case 24 for a revised parameter, retaining meaningful units.\n4. Design a two-site or two-orientation test that distinguishes the interpretations.\n5. Write a short final paragraph reporting what remains uncertain and which measurement would reduce the uncertainty.",
