          "secNumber": "5.7",
          "heading": "Lesson 5.5 \u2014 Fracture mechanics and scaling",
          "content": "### Mechanism and observational scale\n\nFracture-tip stress concentration, material toughness and existing flaw geometry influence propagation; natural joints span multiple orders of scale. Draw a basic geometric sketch and define the coordinate system, sample orientation and reference state if one is needed. \n\n### Evidence and documentation\n\nArrest at layer boundaries, interaction with other cracks and systematic spacing can reveal mechanical contrasts. Document the exposure, instrument or measurement technique, scale and uncertainty before discussing tectonic implications. Maintain an observation/inference table with **scanline orientation, surface exposure window and relative age of mineral fill** and illustrate **joint set frequency, intersection topology and hydraulic uncertainty**. If your evidence is a two-dimensional trace of a three-dimensional surface, state exactly what its projection does and does not resolve. \n\n### Competing interpretation\n\nRegular spacing may arise from sequential saturation or from a joint set preorganized by layering rather than one simple stress event. Record expected results for each explanation before carrying out the test. A model requiring hidden geometry must mark that geometry as inferred.\n\n### Test and scientific protocol\n\nCompare crack-tip termination frequencies across layer interfaces and distinguish size-dependent observation completeness. State the predicted direction, location, quantity or material signature under both models. Use at least one measurement not previously employed to construct the model. For **fracture mechanics and scaling**, define sample selection criteria, coordinate conventions and an acceptable range of observation error. \n\n### Worked numerical case 25: Crack aspect ratio\n\n**Invented classroom inputs:** schematic crack length 8.4 m and mean aperture 0.14 m. Evaluate `8.4/0.14` to obtain **60 ratio**. Write the governing equation separately, substitute the stated values, and check the dimensions and sign convention. For a trigonometric expression identify whether angles are entered in radians or degrees; for percentages identify the reference length or population. \n\nRepeat the calculation after varying the least certain input. Explain whether the change is important relative to plausible field error. If the formula neglects a deformation component, assumes a planar fault or uses a restricted stress model, identify that assumption rather than interpreting the number as exact. Distinguish geometric separation from true displacement, finite strain from its time rate and stress from material strength whenever those concepts arise in the calculation. \n\n### Limitation, cross-check and synthesis\n\nAn elastic crack model ignores inelastic process zones, pore fluids and anisotropic material. Consider the alternative that changes the interpretation most while retaining the measured observations. List which surfaces or fabrics predate which others using direct cut, fold, overprint or growth relations. Leave pairs unordered where no observation establishes priority. Reactivation means a single surface may occur more than once in the event graph. \n\nUse idealized fracture scaling to formulate tests rather than engineering predictions without calibration. A defensible conclusion therefore has five components: observed feature, ideal relation used, leading interpretation, decisive contrary test and residual uncertainty. Indicate whether each is local to the measured exposure or can legitimately be extended across the mapped domain.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Sketch **fracture mechanics and scaling** with orientation or movement arrows and a north or section reference.\n2. List three direct observations and identify a plausible rival mechanism.\n3. Recalculate case 25 for a revised parameter, retaining meaningful units.\n4. Design a two-site or two-orientation test that distinguishes the interpretations.\n5. Write a short final paragraph reporting what remains uncertain and which measurement would reduce the uncertainty.",
