          "secNumber": "6.6",
          "heading": "Lesson 6.4 \u2014 Mylonites and recrystallization mechanisms",
          "content": "### Mechanism and observational scale\n\nDynamic recrystallization and grain-shape preferred orientation can produce fine-grained foliated mylonites in ductile shear zones. Draw a basic geometric sketch and define the coordinate system, sample orientation and reference state if one is needed. \n\n### Evidence and documentation\n\nQuartz subgrains, feldspar porphyroclasts and mineral-specific fabrics may distinguish grain-boundary migration from brittle grain crushing. Document the exposure, instrument or measurement technique, scale and uncertainty before discussing tectonic implications. Maintain an observation/inference table with **orientation of the X\u2013Z section, grain-scale mechanism and strain partitioning** and illustrate **fabric asymmetry, finite strain and deformation phase**. If your evidence is a two-dimensional trace of a three-dimensional surface, state exactly what its projection does and does not resolve. \n\n### Competing interpretation\n\nFine-grained fault rock is not necessarily mylonite; cataclasites can also be strongly foliated. Record expected results for each explanation before carrying out the test. A model requiring hidden geometry must mark that geometry as inferred.\n\n### Test and scientific protocol\n\nDocument microstructural features of at least two minerals and compare their likely deformation conditions with independent thermometry. State the predicted direction, location, quantity or material signature under both models. Use at least one measurement not previously employed to construct the model. For **mylonites and recrystallization mechanisms**, define sample selection criteria, coordinate conventions and an acceptable range of observation error. \n\n### Worked numerical case 29: Grain size reduction\n\n**Invented classroom inputs:** representative original 210 \u00b5m versus dynamically recrystallized 78 \u00b5m grains. Evaluate `100*(210-78)/210` to obtain **62.8571 %**. Write the governing equation separately, substitute the stated values, and check the dimensions and sign convention. For a trigonometric expression identify whether angles are entered in radians or degrees; for percentages identify the reference length or population. \n\nRepeat the calculation after varying the least certain input. Explain whether the change is important relative to plausible field error. If the formula neglects a deformation component, assumes a planar fault or uses a restricted stress model, identify that assumption rather than interpreting the number as exact. Distinguish geometric separation from true displacement, finite strain from its time rate and stress from material strength whenever those concepts arise in the calculation. \n\n### Limitation, cross-check and synthesis\n\nDeformation temperature inference from microstructure is not a precise thermometer without calibration. Consider the alternative that changes the interpretation most while retaining the measured observations. Translate the interpretation between a map, a cross-section and a three-dimensional conceptual diagram. Check that all three representations show compatible orientations and movement directions. A model that works in one section but fails in map view needs revision. \n\nExplain which observations support crystal-plastic deformation versus comminution. A defensible conclusion therefore has five components: observed feature, ideal relation used, leading interpretation, decisive contrary test and residual uncertainty. Indicate whether each is local to the measured exposure or can legitimately be extended across the mapped domain.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Sketch **mylonites and recrystallization mechanisms** with orientation or movement arrows and a north or section reference.\n2. List three direct observations and identify a plausible rival mechanism.\n3. Recalculate case 29 for a revised parameter, retaining meaningful units.\n4. Design a two-site or two-orientation test that distinguishes the interpretations.\n5. Write a short final paragraph reporting what remains uncertain and which measurement would reduce the uncertainty.",
