          "heading": "Lesson 6.5 \u2014 Vorticity and pure versus simple shear",
          "content": "### Mechanism and observational scale\n\nSimple shear involves rotational components; coaxial pure shear can change shape without bulk rigid-body rotation of principal axes in a homogeneous model. Draw a basic geometric sketch and define the coordinate system, sample orientation and reference state if one is needed. \n\n### Evidence and documentation\n\nRotating clasts and asymmetric markers can help constrain flow style, though each responds to its own mechanical contrast. Document the exposure, instrument or measurement technique, scale and uncertainty before discussing tectonic implications. Maintain an observation/inference table with **orientation of the X\u2013Z section, grain-scale mechanism and strain partitioning** and illustrate **fabric asymmetry, finite strain and deformation phase**. If your evidence is a two-dimensional trace of a three-dimensional surface, state exactly what its projection does and does not resolve. \n\n### Competing interpretation\n\nFinite foliation orientation alone cannot discriminate simple from pure shear, nor uniquely recover a vorticity history. Record expected results for each explanation before carrying out the test. A model requiring hidden geometry must mark that geometry as inferred.\n\n### Test and scientific protocol\n\nCompare predicted marker rotations under end-member shear simulations and identify which observations would discriminate them. State the predicted direction, location, quantity or material signature under both models. Use at least one measurement not previously employed to construct the model. For **vorticity and pure versus simple shear**, define sample selection criteria, coordinate conventions and an acceptable range of observation error. \n\n### Worked numerical case 30: Ideal pure shear area preservation\n\n**Invented classroom inputs:** two principal stretches 1.4 and reciprocal under 2-D area-preserving idealization. Evaluate `1.4*(1/1.4)` to obtain **1 ratio**. Write the governing equation separately, substitute the stated values, and check the dimensions and sign convention. For a trigonometric expression identify whether angles are entered in radians or degrees; for percentages identify the reference length or population. \n\nRepeat the calculation after varying the least certain input. Explain whether the change is important relative to plausible field error. If the formula neglects a deformation component, assumes a planar fault or uses a restricted stress model, identify that assumption rather than interpreting the number as exact. Distinguish geometric separation from true displacement, finite strain from its time rate and stress from material strength whenever those concepts arise in the calculation. \n\n### Limitation, cross-check and synthesis\n\nMany mixed-flow paths produce equivalent finite strain ellipses. Consider the alternative that changes the interpretation most while retaining the measured observations. List which surfaces or fabrics predate which others using direct cut, fold, overprint or growth relations. Leave pairs unordered where no observation establishes priority. Reactivation means a single surface may occur more than once in the event graph. \n\nA flow-model label should always declare whether it concerns instantaneous kinematics or finite strain. A defensible conclusion therefore has five components: observed feature, ideal relation used, leading interpretation, decisive contrary test and residual uncertainty. Indicate whether each is local to the measured exposure or can legitimately be extended across the mapped domain.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Sketch **vorticity and pure versus simple shear** with orientation or movement arrows and a north or section reference.\n2. List three direct observations and identify a plausible rival mechanism.\n3. Recalculate case 30 for a revised parameter, retaining meaningful units.\n4. Design a two-site or two-orientation test that distinguishes the interpretations.\n5. Write a short final paragraph reporting what remains uncertain and which measurement would reduce the uncertainty.",
