          "secNumber": "7.2",
          "heading": "Extended unit perspective",
          "content": "Regional structural reconstruction links geometrical measurements into a chronological sequence. This often means making two cross-sections that honour the same sparse observations but differ in their interpretation of the hidden subsurface. An apparently balanced section can still be wrong if its pin line is incorrect, if stratigraphic thickness varies laterally or if a detachment is placed for convenience rather than evidence. Similarly, extension across a rift cannot automatically be computed from the sum of apparent throws on one section. The geometry must be compatible with the strike of faults, plunge of markers, rotation of blocks and the selected restoration method. In contractional settings, fault-bend and fault-propagation folds predict different hinge histories; growth strata may discriminate them if deposition is dated. In strike-slip settings, transpression and transtension can produce complicated mixtures of folds and faults even where the overall displacement is lateral. The scientific objective is not to select the most visually persuasive tectonic picture, but to state predictions before consulting independent observations. An effective regional study contains a data inventory, scale, explicit uncertainty range, alternative geometry and criteria by which a new measurement would overturn the model.\n\n### Field and scientific objectives\n\nDescribe relevant structural objects with location and orientation, translate them between map and section, construct a physically and chronologically consistent interpretation, and test at least one alternative. The aim is to communicate exactly which information is observed, which result follows by geometric calculation and which regional mechanism is inferred. Avoid interpreting one structural indicator as proof of a single tectonic event where earlier deformation, reactivation or anisotropic rock properties remain plausible.",
          "simulations": []
