          "secNumber": "7.5",
          "heading": "Lesson 7.3 \u2014 Extensional basin architecture",
          "content": "### Mechanism and observational scale\n\nNormal fault displacement and rotation create tilted blocks, accommodation variation and stratigraphic wedges within rifts. Draw a basic geometric sketch and define the coordinate system, sample orientation and reference state if one is needed. \n\n### Evidence and documentation\n\nGrowth strata thickening toward an active fault, onlap surfaces and footwall uplift patterns support syn-depositional movement. Document the exposure, instrument or measurement technique, scale and uncertainty before discussing tectonic implications. Maintain an observation/inference table with **pin-line assumptions, section azimuth and independent subsurface constraints** and illustrate **unrestored section, restored section and held-out prediction**. If your evidence is a two-dimensional trace of a three-dimensional surface, state exactly what its projection does and does not resolve. \n\n### Competing interpretation\n\nDifferential compaction or sediment supply changes can also create thickness asymmetry without active faulting. Record expected results for each explanation before carrying out the test. A model requiring hidden geometry must mark that geometry as inferred.\n\n### Test and scientific protocol\n\nMeasure wedge thickness and dated horizons along transects and restore rotation separately from displacement. State the predicted direction, location, quantity or material signature under both models. Use at least one measurement not previously employed to construct the model. For **extensional basin architecture**, define sample selection criteria, coordinate conventions and an acceptable range of observation error. \n\n### Worked numerical case 33: Normal-fault throw in mapped section\n\n**Invented classroom inputs:** fault slip 65 m along 56\u00b0 dipping plane, pure dip-slip assumption. Evaluate `65*math.sin(math.radians(56))` to obtain **53.8874 m**. Write the governing equation separately, substitute the stated values, and check the dimensions and sign convention. For a trigonometric expression identify whether angles are entered in radians or degrees; for percentages identify the reference length or population. \n\nRepeat the calculation after varying the least certain input. Explain whether the change is important relative to plausible field error. If the formula neglects a deformation component, assumes a planar fault or uses a restricted stress model, identify that assumption rather than interpreting the number as exact. Distinguish geometric separation from true displacement, finite strain from its time rate and stress from material strength whenever those concepts arise in the calculation. \n\n### Limitation, cross-check and synthesis\n\nBlind faults and post-rift inversion can obscure initial extension geometry. Consider the alternative that changes the interpretation most while retaining the measured observations. Identify the least certain input, vary it by a physically plausible amount, and show its effect on the computed result. Variation in a trigonometric angle may produce a nonlinear uncertainty even where angle readings appear precise. Report significant figures in proportion to the instrument and assumptions. \n\nRequire age-constrained growth relations to distinguish syn-depositional tectonics from later deformation. A defensible conclusion therefore has five components: observed feature, ideal relation used, leading interpretation, decisive contrary test and residual uncertainty. Indicate whether each is local to the measured exposure or can legitimately be extended across the mapped domain.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Sketch **extensional basin architecture** with orientation or movement arrows and a north or section reference.\n2. List three direct observations and identify a plausible rival mechanism.\n3. Recalculate case 33 for a revised parameter, retaining meaningful units.\n4. Design a two-site or two-orientation test that distinguishes the interpretations.\n5. Write a short final paragraph reporting what remains uncertain and which measurement would reduce the uncertainty.",
