          "secNumber": "7.7",
          "heading": "Lesson 7.5 \u2014 Structural uncertainty, hazards and responsible reporting",
          "content": "### Mechanism and observational scale\n\nGeologic interpretations are inferences constrained by observations; mapping accuracy and uncertainty affect hazard and subsurface decisions. Draw a basic geometric sketch and define the coordinate system, sample orientation and reference state if one is needed. \n\n### Evidence and documentation\n\nCompeting cross sections, model residuals, paleoseismic evidence and instrument limitations establish confidence in different claims. Document the exposure, instrument or measurement technique, scale and uncertainty before discussing tectonic implications. Maintain an observation/inference table with **pin-line assumptions, section azimuth and independent subsurface constraints** and illustrate **unrestored section, restored section and held-out prediction**. If your evidence is a two-dimensional trace of a three-dimensional surface, state exactly what its projection does and does not resolve. \n\n### Competing interpretation\n\nA precise-looking map can still be inaccurate because its data are sparse, selected or from incompatible times. Record expected results for each explanation before carrying out the test. A model requiring hidden geometry must mark that geometry as inferred.\n\n### Test and scientific protocol\n\nWithhold one independent observation, predict it under each model, update the interpretation and document remaining ambiguity. State the predicted direction, location, quantity or material signature under both models. Use at least one measurement not previously employed to construct the model. For **structural uncertainty, hazards and responsible reporting**, define sample selection criteria, coordinate conventions and an acceptable range of observation error. \n\n### Worked numerical case 35: Mean marker-offset rate\n\n**Invented classroom inputs:** 32 m displacement over 1.6 Myr, expressed numerically as mm per kyr. Evaluate `32/1.6` to obtain **20 mm/kyr**. Write the governing equation separately, substitute the stated values, and check the dimensions and sign convention. For a trigonometric expression identify whether angles are entered in radians or degrees; for percentages identify the reference length or population. \n\nRepeat the calculation after varying the least certain input. Explain whether the change is important relative to plausible field error. If the formula neglects a deformation component, assumes a planar fault or uses a restricted stress model, identify that assumption rather than interpreting the number as exact. Distinguish geometric separation from true displacement, finite strain from its time rate and stress from material strength whenever those concepts arise in the calculation. \n\n### Limitation, cross-check and synthesis\n\nClassroom displacement-rate and strength models are never adequate for operational earthquake, tunnel or slope-safety decisions. Consider the alternative that changes the interpretation most while retaining the measured observations. List which surfaces or fabrics predate which others using direct cut, fold, overprint or growth relations. Leave pairs unordered where no observation establishes priority. Reactivation means a single surface may occur more than once in the event graph. \n\nReport assumptions, reproducibility, alternatives and uncertainty before recommending an application. A defensible conclusion therefore has five components: observed feature, ideal relation used, leading interpretation, decisive contrary test and residual uncertainty. Indicate whether each is local to the measured exposure or can legitimately be extended across the mapped domain.\n\n### Practice\n\n1. Sketch **structural uncertainty, hazards and responsible reporting** with orientation or movement arrows and a north or section reference.\n2. List three direct observations and identify a plausible rival mechanism.\n3. Recalculate case 35 for a revised parameter, retaining meaningful units.\n4. Design a two-site or two-orientation test that distinguishes the interpretations.\n5. Write a short final paragraph reporting what remains uncertain and which measurement would reduce the uncertainty.",
